1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,808
Right now, five NASA satellites are circling the Earth, watching for fire.

2
00:00:05,188 --> 00:00:09,183
In the last day alone, they reported tens of thousands of hot spots.

3
00:00:09,562 --> 00:00:12,568
Two of these satellites have watched for more than twenty years.

4
00:00:12,948 --> 00:00:14,824
But they do not see fire the same way.

5
00:00:15,723 --> 00:00:20,300
We are Team Lumos, from Dhaka: M Al Shahriar Hameem, our team lead,

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:26,266
with Tuasin Taj Fiha, Afifa Asif, Md Abu Ammar, Kausar Ahmed and Sabiha Akter.

7
00:00:26,646 --> 00:00:29,993
For the NASA Space Apps Challenge, we built Parallax Fire,

8
00:00:30,193 --> 00:00:35,509
to turn two satellite records that disagree into one honest story of when a place burns.

9
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:41,043
The older satellite, MODIS, sees the ground in pixels one kilometre wide.

10
00:00:41,423 --> 00:00:48,146
The sharper one, VIIRS, arrived in 2012 and sees pixels of 375 metres,

11
00:00:48,346 --> 00:00:49,929
so it catches smaller fires.

12
00:00:50,309 --> 00:00:53,391
In Bangladesh it records about five times as many.

13
00:00:54,291 --> 00:00:58,195
So when you join the two records, burning seems to leap in 2012.

14
00:00:58,575 --> 00:01:01,546
Imagine planning your district’s fire season from this chart.

15
00:01:01,926 --> 00:01:05,650
You would ask for help against a change that never happened on the ground.

16
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:11,237
Parallax Fire puts both on one scale. It learns how the two satellites relate,

17
00:01:11,437 --> 00:01:14,601
only from years when both were flying. Press one switch,

18
00:01:14,801 --> 00:01:18,913
and most of that jump disappears. The camera changed, not the fire.

19
00:01:19,813 --> 00:01:22,849
Here is a real test. In March 2024,

20
00:01:23,049 --> 00:01:27,984
the sharper satellite counted 621 detections over Bangladesh.

21
00:01:28,364 --> 00:01:34,003
The older one counted 111. That month was never used to build our estimate.

22
00:01:34,383 --> 00:01:38,704
From the older count alone, we estimated about 1,093,

23
00:01:38,904 --> 00:01:47,661
likely between 571 and 1,529. The real 621 fell inside that range.

24
00:01:48,561 --> 00:01:49,755
First, pick a place.

25
00:01:50,135 --> 00:01:53,806
The calendar shows each year as a row and each month as a column.

26
00:01:54,186 --> 00:01:58,201
In Bangladesh, March and April hold about four in five detections,

27
00:01:58,401 --> 00:02:02,445
year after year. In California, the season moves to late summer.

28
00:02:03,345 --> 00:02:07,586
Next, the map shows where it burned, district by district, on a real map.

29
00:02:08,486 --> 00:02:13,722
We checked the method on the years 2023 and 2024, which it had never seen.

30
00:02:14,102 --> 00:02:17,267
In Bangladesh it missed by 98 detections a month,

31
00:02:17,467 --> 00:02:21,048
where the raw older count misses by 627.

32
00:02:21,428 --> 00:02:23,589
In New South Wales it was not good enough,

33
00:02:23,789 --> 00:02:26,732
so there the app shows the raw records only, and says so.

34
00:02:27,633 --> 00:02:28,922
Finally, for today,

35
00:02:29,122 --> 00:02:32,287
the live Earth shows each fire satellite where it really is,

36
00:02:32,487 --> 00:02:35,970
with every hot spot it reported in the last twenty-four hours.

37
00:02:36,350 --> 00:02:40,506
It is all NASA data: FIRMS fire detections from MODIS and VIIRS,

38
00:02:40,706 --> 00:02:44,036
and GIBS satellite imagery, with public orbit records.

39
00:02:44,936 --> 00:02:49,473
A planner can see the real burning season before deciding when to watch closely.

40
00:02:49,853 --> 00:02:54,472
A researcher can compare two decades without mistaking a new camera for new fire.

41
00:02:54,852 --> 00:02:58,448
Looking ahead, we want more regions, the newer NOAA satellites,

42
00:02:58,648 --> 00:03:01,303
a Bangla interface, and partners to test it with.

43
00:03:02,203 --> 00:03:07,685
Parallax Fire. Same fires, two cameras, one honest record. This is Team Lumos.

44
00:03:08,065 --> 00:03:08,736
Thank you.
